Based on my knowledge, here's the answer to your sub-question:

The title of the 1975 film adaptation of Ken Kesey's novel 'One Flew Over the Cuckoo's Nest' is the same as the novel, 'One Flew Over the Cuckoo's Nest'. The film was directed by Miloš Forman.

Miloš Forman was a Czech-American film director, screenwriter, and professor. Before directing 'One Flew Over the Cuckoo's Nest', he had already gained recognition for his work on several films, including 'Black Peter' (1964), 'Loves of a Blonde' (1965), and 'The Firemen's Ball' (1967). He had also directed several other notable films, such as 'Taking Off' (1971) and 'Hair' (1979).

The film 'One Flew Over the Cuckoo's Nest' received several notable awards, including five Academy Awards: Best Picture, Best Director (Miloš Forman), Best Actor (Jack Nicholson), Best Actress (Louise Fletcher), and Best Adapted Screenplay (Lawrence Hauben and Bo Goldman). It also won four Golden Globe Awards and four BAFTA Awards.

The film's impact on the film industry and popular culture was significant. It helped establish Jack Nicholson as a leading actor and cemented Miloš Forman's reputation as a master director. The film's portrayal of the struggles of mental health patients and the abuse of power in institutions sparked important conversations about social justice and human rights. It also influenced the development of future films and TV shows that tackled similar themes.
