What is the historical context of the term 'Catholic' in early Christianity?
The term "Catholic" in early Christianity originated in the 2nd century to emphasize the universal scope of the Christian Church. It was first used to describe the Christian Church in the early 2nd century, and was initially used to distinguish it from other Christian groups. The term was derived from the Greek phrase "katholou," meaning "on the whole" or "according to the whole," and was used to convey the idea of a unified and universal Christian community.
