What are the current records of the deepest ocean depths, including the location and method of measurement, and what are the limitations of these measurements?
The current record of the deepest ocean depth is held by the Challenger Deep, located in the Mariana Trench near the Philippines in the Pacific Ocean. According to the 2010 mapping conducted by the US Center for Coastal & Ocean Mapping/Joint Hydrographic Center (CCOM/JHC) aboard the ship, the location of the deepest depth recorded is at 11°22′ N latitude and 142°35′ E longitude, with a depth of approximately 10,973 meters (36,037 feet).

The depth measurement uncertainty is estimated to be ± 40 meters (95% confidence level) on 9 degrees of freedom, and a positional uncertainty of ± 2 meters (2drms) remains. This uncertainty is due to various factors, including the spatial variations in sound-speed through the water volume, the ray-tracing and bottom-detection algorithms of the multibeam system, the accuracies and calibration of the motion sensor and navigation systems, estimates of spherical spreading, and attenuation throughout the water volume.

It's worth noting that the 2009 and 2010 maximal depths were not confirmed by subsequent measurements, and the 2016 sonar mapping of the Challenger Deep area conducted by the Royal Netherlands Institute for Sea Research (NIOZ)/GEOMAR Helmholtz Centre for Ocean Research Kiel aboard RV "Sonne" using a Kongsberg Maritime EM 122 multibeam echosounder system did not provide a new record depth.
