How did the warning labels on cigarette packs evolve over time, including any changes in design, content, or regulations across different countries?
Based on the provided context, here's a clear and concise answer to the sub-question:

Warning labels on cigarette packs have evolved over time, with changes in design, content, and regulations across different countries. Initially, textual warnings were used, but later, graphic visual images were introduced to make the warnings more effective. The content of the warnings has also changed, with some countries using disturbing images of tobacco-related harms, such as heart disease, lung cancer, and mouth cancer. Regulations have also been implemented, such as the Federal Cigarette Labeling and Advertising Act (FCLAA) in the United States in 1965, requiring warning labels on cigarette packs. Today, many countries have both textual warnings and graphic visual images displaying the damaging effects of tobacco use on the human body.
