What is the history of daylight saving time in the United States, including the first implementation and any subsequent changes?
The first implementation of daylight saving time (DST) in the United States was in 1918, when the Standard Time Act of 1918, also known as the Calder Act, was passed. This act authorized the Interstate Commerce Commission to define each time zone and implemented DST. However, the section concerning DST was repealed by the act titled "An Act For the repeal of the daylight-saving law" over President Woodrow Wilson's veto.

The first jurisdiction in North America to adopt DST was the Dominion of Newfoundland, which enacted the Daylight Saving Act of 1917 in 1917, a year after the United Kingdom.

In the United States, DST was not reinstated until World War II, when the Uniform Time Act of 1966 was passed, which standardized DST across the country. However, the start and end dates of DST have been changed several times since then, with the most recent change being the Energy Policy Act of 2005, which extended the duration of DST by four weeks.

It's worth noting that the Standard Time Act of 1918 also mistakenly placed most of the state of Idaho in Central Standard Time, but this was amended in 2007.
