When did the Uniform Time Act of 1966 take effect, and how did it impact daylight saving time in the United States?
The Uniform Time Act of 1966 took effect in 1967. It mandated that daylight saving time (DST) begin nationwide on the last Sunday in April and end on the last Sunday in October. The act preempted all previously enacted state laws related to the beginning and ending of DST, establishing a uniform national policy for DST.
