What is the historical context of hydrogen fuel cell development, and what were the key milestones in the early years of research and experimentation with hydrogen fuel cells, including any notable events, discoveries, or publications that contributed to the understanding and development of hydrogen fuel cells as a viable energy source.
The historical context of hydrogen fuel cell development dates back to 1838, when Christian Friedrich Schönbein discovered the principle of a fuel cell. The first fuel cell was constructed by Sir William Robert Grove in 1839, which was similar to today's phosphoric acid fuel cells. Grove's fuel cell used a combination of sheet iron, copper, and porcelain plates, and a solution.

A notable event in the early years of hydrogen fuel cell research was the publication of a letter by William Grove in the December 1838 edition of "The London and Edinburgh Philosophical Magazine and Journal of Science". In this letter, Grove described his development of the first crude fuel cells.

The early years of hydrogen fuel cell research were marked by experimentation and discovery, with scientists exploring the potential of hydrogen as a fuel source. The development of hydrogen fuel cells was a gradual process, with key milestones including the discovery of the principle of a fuel cell by Schönbein in 1838 and the construction of the first fuel cell by Grove in 1839.
